# Receiving JSON 📥

Add the model to your path operation as a parameter. FastAPI will automatically read the body of the HTTP request as JSON, convert it to the Pydantic model, and validate it!

```python_not
from fastapi import FastAPI
app = FastAPI()

@app.post("/items/")
def create_item(item: Item):
    # 'item' is now a fully instantiated Pydantic object!
    total_price = item.price
    if item.tax:
        total_price += item.tax

    return {"message": f"{item.name} costs {total_price}"}
```

# The 422 Unprocessable Entity 🚫

If a client sends this JSON:
```json
{
    "name": "Laptop",
    "price": "expensive"
}
```

Because `price` must be a float, FastAPI intercepts the request *before* it even reaches your function!
It returns a helpful `422 Unprocessable Entity` error telling the client exactly what went wrong:

```json
{
  "detail": [
    {
      "loc": ["body", "price"],
      "msg": "value is not a valid float",
      "type": "type_error.float"
    }
  ]
}
```

# Output Validation 📤

Pydantic isn't just for incoming data. It's for outgoing data too!
You can declare a `response_model` in your path operation.

```python_not
class UserOut(BaseModel):
    username: str
    email: str

@app.get("/user/", response_model=UserOut)
def get_user():
    # Even if this dict has extra fields (like a password)
    # FastAPI will filter them out before sending the response!
    return {"username": "admin", "email": "admin@a.com", "password": "secret"}
```
